]\n\nIngredient groups\n\nName  | ChEBI-ID  | CAS Registry Number®  | Formula  | Mass [g/mol]\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nToray silicone SH 5535  |  |  |  | \n\nShow 1 to 1 (1 total)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFormulary (https://www.nottinghamshireformulary.nhs.uk/reports/Archive/A0003.asp)\nciteturn26search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 05.02 Amphotericin B (non lipid formulation) Fungizone^{®} ...   * Brand Fungizone®\n  * Also known as Amphotericin B (as sodium deoxycholate complex)\n\n  * Brand Fungizone®\n  * Also known as Amphotericin B (as sodium deoxycholate complex)\n  * Fungizone® is a conventional formulation; it is NOT interchangeable with other formulations of amphotericin B such as Amphotericin B Lipsomal (AmBisome or generic).\nAt NUH - highly restricted use due to risk of fatal consequences if incorrect formulation is used\n\n  * In Ophthalmology/ENT:\n    * Eye/ear drop preparation (0.15%) - unlicensed preparation, ordered on a named patient basis via outpatients / dispensaries (not stocked).\n    * Intravitreal injection is Amphotericin B 15 microgram in 0.3ml inj (dose is 5 micrograms in 0.1mL, the rest is overage), this is not stocked and made during normal opening hours by SPU.\n  * All other preparations e.g. intrathecal - supply is stricted restricted to Pharmacy Sterile Production Unit only \n  * Use via intrathecal route is on the advice of consultant microbiologist only, and made by SPU only\n  * NB: Usually, Amphotericin B LIPOSOMAL preparations are used for IV administration due to reduced nephrotoxicity (see guidance)\n20 Amphotericin B 500mg in 5ml oral soln  Unlicensed medicine - named patient use only\nNUH only\n05.02 Amphotericin B Liposomal  Infusion (liposomal formulation)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupplemental Report For Video Training Tape On Ceriodaphnia Survival And Reproduction Toxicity Tests (https://nepis.epa.gov/Exe/ZyPURL.cgi?Dockey=94004TCH.TXT)\nciteturn26search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 38.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;     test medium. ...     Cerophyll® (Powdered, Dried, Cereal Leaves)' :\n    South, Murray, Utah. Mix well in a blender and aerate continuously\n    (digest) for one week at ambient laboratory temperature prior to use.\n    Be sure to aerate from the bottom to prevent settling, and replace water\n    lost to evaporation during fermentation.\n    2.\tAfter one week of digesting, place in a refrigerator and allo.w the\n    mixture to settle. Decant the supernatant through a #110 Nytex® screen\n    and combine with equal volumes of supernatant from the Cerophyll® and\n    yeast preparations (below). The liquid can be frozen for use later.\n    Cerophyll® (Powdered, Dried, Cereal Leaves)' :\n    1.\tOne day prior to use, place 5.0 g of Cerophyll® powder in a flask. Add\n    500 ml of distilled, deionized water or water used for culturing. Mix at\n    high speed for five minutes in a blender or stir with a magnetic stirrer\n    at medium overnight.\n    2.\tIf blended, store in refrigerator overnight.\n    3.\tDecant the supernatant through a #110 Nytex® screen and combine with\n    equal volumes of supernant from trout chow (above) and yeast\n    preparations (below). Discard any excess.\n    Available from Sigma Chemical Company, P.O. Box 14508, St. Louis,\n    Missouri, 63178 (800-325-3010).\n    \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEfficacy and toxicity evaluation of new amphotericin B micelle systems for brain fungal infections (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378517315301009)\nciteturn26search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; AmB deoxycholate (Fungizone^{®} 1 mg/kg/day), and liposomal AmB (AmBisome^{®} 5 mg/kg/day) exhibited a good distribution access to the central nervous system (CNS) and led to the complete eradication of Candida albicans from the brain (Clemons et al., 2005, Shao et al., 2010). ... Amphotericin B (AmB) and Fungizone^{®} (commercial formulations containing AmB and NaDC) were supplied by Bristol Myers, Squibb (Barcelona, Spain).\nSelected formulations were also tested in a murine model of systemic candidiasis to assess their efficacy in brain fungal infections.\n\n### Materials\n\nAmphotericin B (AmB) and Fungizone^{®} (commercial formulations containing AmB and NaDC) were supplied by Bristol Myers, Squibb (Barcelona, Spain). AmBisome^{®} (commercial liposomal formulation containing AmB, phosphatidylcholine, cholesterol and distearoylphosphatidylglycerol) was supplied by UCB-Pharma (Brussels, Belgium). Sodium deoxycholate was purchased from Fluka-Biochemika^{®} (Buchs, Switzerland). All other chemical reagents were of pharmaceutical grade or better.\n\n### Candida albicans strain\n\nC. albicans SC5314 (Gillum et\n\n### Aggregation state of amphotericin B\n\nFungizone^{®} was a clear yellow solution, Ambisome^{®} was a translucent yellowish formulation easily dispersible in water. Dimeric AmB formulations, such as Fungizone^{®}, exhibit a clear yellow solution appearance while partially poly-aggregated AmB formulations, such as Ambisome^{®}, show a translucent yellowish appearance (Serrano et al., 2013).\n\nThe prepared poly-aggregated micelle formulations were yellowish freeze-dried powders that were highly dispersible in water and produced an opaque yellow\n\n### Conclusions\n\nThe aggregation state and the NaDC content in the micellar NaDC systems may explain the differences in their distribution, and therefore in their toxicity and efficacy compared to the references formulations of Fungizone^{®} and AmBisome^{®}. AmB:NaDC 1:0.8 and 1:1.5 nano-sized micelle systems showed a poly-aggregated form of AmB. These micellar poly-aggregated systems have lower acute toxicity than Fungizone^{®}.\n\nDifferences on brain antifungal activities were related to the NaDC content in the micellar\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn-vivo therapeutic efficacy in experimental murine mycoses of a new formulation of deoxycholate-amphotericin B obtained by mild heating. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10052902/)\nciteturn26search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Heat-induced 'superaggregation' of deoxycholate-amphotericin B (AmB-DOC, Fungizone) was shown previously to reduce the in-vitro toxicity of this antifungal agent. We compared AmB-DOC with the formulation obtained by heating the commercial form (Fungizone, Bristol Myers Squibb, Paris, France) for 20 min at 70 degrees C, in the treatment of murine infections.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColpoda Toxicity (https://www.nies.go.jp/chiiki1/protoz/toxicity/protozoa/colpoda.htm)\nciteturn26search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5.7 years ago; 24-well polystyrene tissue culture plates (medium: 10% Sonneborn's medium (Sonneborn's medium was prepared by boiling 2.5 g cerophyll (cereal leaves) for 5 min in 1 L of distilled, deionized water.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelivery strategies of amphotericin B for invasive fungal infections (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8424280/)\nciteturn26search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: last week; The representative marketed product is FUNGIZONE®^{140}, in which sodium deoxycholate is employed as a solubilizer to overcome the poor aqueous solubility of AmB.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Pharmacokinetics, Tissue Distributions, and Effects on Renal Function of Novel Polymeric Formulations of Amphotericin B and Amphotericin B-Deoxycholate in Rats (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC89789/)\nciteturn26search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 26.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; AmB was kindly given by Bristol-Myers Squibb (Madrid, Spain), and piroxicam (internal standard) was supplied by Boral Química (Barcelona, Spain); desoxycholate-AmB (Fungizone) was purchased from Bristol-Myers Squibb (Madrid, Spain). ... Fungizone (deoxycholate-AmB) was reconstituted with sterile water and was further diluted with a 5% glucose–water solution.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISO 20665 - 2008-12 - DIN Media (https://www.dinmedia.de/de/norm/iso-20665/115723552)\nciteturn26search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.9 years ago; Crawled: yesterday;   * Preparation of the ELENDT M4 medium (normative) Unterkapitel anzeigen Unterkapitel ausblenden ...     * Preparing Cerophyll (dried, powdered cereal leaves)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvaluation of the efficacy and toxicity of amphotericin B incorporated in lipid nano-sphere (LNS®) (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378517303003429)\nciteturn26search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Fungizone, a colloidal dispersion of AmB in a micellar solution of deoxycholate, was obtained from Bristol-Myers Squibb (Princeton, NJ, USA).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn26view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI:132110\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/api/ontologies/chebi/terms?obo_id=CHEBI%3A132110 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn26view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:132110\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI%3A132110 is not accessible via this tool.----------------------------------------------------